  "query_preview": "..\nDisturbances within the monsoon trough are promoting an active\nweather regime just north and west of the Marianas this afternoon,\nwhile the southernmost islands experience a relative lull. Moderate \nto strong westerlies are expected to continue across the area over \nthe next few days, increasing Sunday through Monday as the remnants \nof 13W swing east-southeastward, dipping over or near the far \nnorthern CNMI leading to a resurgence in monsoon winds across the \narea. Model guidance shows the southern edge of the developing \nmonsoon surge shifting over Tinian and Saipan, maintaining breezy \nconditions and scattered showers there for much of the coming week.\nA major factor in the short-term forecast concerns the remnants of \n13W, which are currently rated as \"low\" for development by the Joint \nTyphoon Warning Center (JTWC), meaning 13W is being monitored but is \nnot expected to develop into a significant tropical cyclone in the \nnext day or so. Some deterministic models do show 13W quickly \ndeveloping north of the Marianas before it races off to the east- \nnortheast, following behind Invest 98W. The magnitude and rate of \n13W's development, as well as how far south its track takes it, will \nbe the main influencing factors in the strength of the monsoon surge \nacross the CNMI over the next few days.",
